# CreativeEngine — handover to Claude

Prepared: 2026-10-14 (America/Los_Angeles). Replaces the 2026-09-12 handover.
Workspace: `~/Documents/AI_experiments/CreativeEngine` (cloned from GitHub on 2026-10-14; the older `~/Documents/AI_Traffik_Projects/CreativeEngine` path is no longer the working copy).

## Purpose and limits of this handover

This file orients a new session. It is a snapshot reconstructed from the repository and its git history, not a memory of earlier conversations. It authorizes no edit, selection, delivery or publishing on its own. If the user's message names a task, that wins; if it doesn't, ask which piece of work they want next.

## Start here

1. Check the branch: `git switch creative-engine-v1 && git pull`. `main` holds only a `.gitignore` (the initial commit); all the work is on `creative-engine-v1`.
2. Read `CLAUDE.md` (constitution and routing) and `WORKING-PREFERENCES.md` (Andres's standing rules). Both apply to every role on every machine.
3. For client work, read `projects/README.md`, then that client's `client/README.md`, then the project's `README.md` and its normalized brief, if there is one.
4. Run `git status` and `git log -5` before editing: another machine or session may have pushed since this snapshot.

## Repository state

- GitHub: private repo `CreativeEngine`. Branches: `main` (empty), `creative-engine-v1` (current work, 66 commits, 2026-09-10 to 2026-10-14), `handoff` (see below).
- Latest commit on `creative-engine-v1`: `9fd8e01 Task Hub: Claude builder prompt for Leo` (2026-10-14).
- It is a Markdown creative operating system and client-work archive, not a software application. There is no build or test command. HTML files are hi-fi prototypes.
- Large media stays out of git (`.gitignore`): `*.mp4`, `*.mov`, `*.zip`, generated media under `projects/**/work/generated/` (only the Markdown logs are committed), and `projects/**/assets/video/`. Where a project has large originals, its `raw/LARGE-FILES.md` says where they live (client Drive or Dropbox).
- `handoff` branch: `creative-engine-v1` plus a `handoff/` folder, a shared inbox so two Claude sessions on different machines can pass work through GitHub (`to-A.md`, `to-B.md`, append-only). Read `handoff/README.md` on that branch only when the user asks to "check the handoff".

## Architecture (short version; `CLAUDE.md` is the authority)

- Five roles, each defined in `<Role>/AGENT.md`, with thin wrappers in `.claude/agents/`: BriefMachine (`brief-machine`), DesignChamp (`design-champ`), WordSmither (`word-smither`), SprintFacilitator (`sprint-facilitator`), CreativeDirector (`creative-engine-director`).
- Status: CreativeDirector, WordSmither and SprintFacilitator are full V1 definitions. BriefMachine and DesignChamp are still placeholders.
- Modes: EXPLORE, DEVELOP, JUDGE. Verdicts: KILL, REWORK, ADVANCE, BET THE PITCH.
- Frameworks: 15 active in `shared/frameworks/`, registered in `CLAUDE.md` (the registry is the only list of what exists). `animation-craft.md` is planned, not written. Barry's *The Advertising Concept Book* is the default working approach.
- Skills: about 40 approved skills in `.claude/skills/`, listed with owners in `.claude/skills/README.md`. A department's skill record governs how that skill is used (for example `WordSmither/skills/humanizer.md`). No third-party skill is added without the review in `CLAUDE.md` and the user's approval.
- Knowledge: global method in `shared/` and department `knowledge/`; client facts only in `projects/<client>/`. One client's workspace is never evidence for another's. Raw sources are never edited.

## Standing rules worth repeating

From `WORKING-PREFERENCES.md`; read the file for the full list and dates.

- Humanize anything that goes out under Andres's name.
- Never remove Andres's own design work; flag and propose instead.
- "Elevate this deck" means slide text stays verbatim; copy ideas go in speaker notes as suggestions.
- Hi-fi prototypes, never production code (the one exception is native Instapage pages through `instapage-production`).
- Global kits stay client-agnostic.

